card('2-139','Distinguish the likely bonding in solids P and Q: P conducts only after melting; Q conducts while solid and can be drawn into wire. Explain the charge-carrier evidence.','P is ionic: ions become mobile on melting. Q is metallic: mobile electrons conduct in the solid.','P ionic, ions move on melting (1).|Q metallic, mobile electrons conduct while solid (1).',ao=['B'],sub='INTERP',bloom='Analyse',recall=0)
card('6-001','Distinguish the changes in two closed systems: X is a liquid cooled to a solid with unchanged molecular identities; Y is heated and its original particles are replaced by two different substances, with total mass unchanged.','X is physical because identity is unchanged. Y is chemical because new substances form; mass conservation does not make it physical.','X physical, same molecular identity (1).|Y chemical, new substances despite conserved total mass (1).',ao=['B'],sub='INTERP',bloom='Analyse',recall=0)
card('9-023','Describe which steelmaking route fits each feed and identify the incompatible alternative. X supplies molten iron rich in carbon but little electricity; Y supplies solid scrap with electrical power. The available routes are oxygen blowing into molten iron and an electric-arc furnace.','X fits oxygen blowing: oxygen removes excess carbon from the molten feed. Y fits the electric arc: electrical energy melts solid scrap; oxygen blowing alone is not a heat source for this solid feed.','X oxygen blowing, removing excess carbon from molten iron (1).|Y electric arc, using electricity to melt solid scrap (1).',ao=['B'],sub='APP',bloom='Apply',recall=0)
card('12-005','Describe the reliability and calibration issue shown by three readings of a 50.0 °C reference: 52.0, 52.0 and 52.0 °C. State whether averaging would fix the issue.','The results are repeatable but consistently 2.0 °C high. Averaging gives the same offset; calibration/correction is needed.','Repeatable results have a +2.0 °C systematic offset (1).|Averaging leaves the offset, so check calibration/correct it (1).',ao=['C'],sub='PROC',bloom='Analyse',recall=0)
# Broader applied environmental comparisons replace standalone lists, without supplying their conclusions.
card('11-003','Discuss the more secure water source from these records and a limitation of the comparison: dam X loses 25% of storage each dry season with no dry-season inflow; aquifer Y has annual recharge 4 million m³ and proposed abstraction 6 million m³.','Neither is secure from these figures alone. X loses water without replenishment during the dry season. Y’s abstraction exceeds recharge by 2 million m³/year. Initial storage, demand and water quality are needed for a final decision.','X’s dry-season loss without inflow threatens storage (1).|Y abstracts 2 million m³/year more than recharge (1).|A final choice needs storage/demand/quality evidence (1).',ao=['B'],sub='LIM',bloom='Evaluate',recall=0)
card('11-024','Discuss the priority investigation after a river survey: site A upstream has dissolved oxygen 8 mg/dm³ and low bacteria; B below a sewage outlet has 3 mg/dm³ and high bacteria; C near a landfill has 8 mg/dm³ but unusually high dissolved contaminants.','Investigate sewage inputs at B because bacterial contamination and oxygen depletion coincide below the outlet; investigate leachate at C because normal oxygen does not exclude dissolved pollution. The spatial association alone does not establish the sources conclusively.','B’s bacteria and lower oxygen support investigating sewage (1).|C’s dissolved contaminants warrant leachate testing despite oxygen 8 mg/dm³ (1).|Repeat/source-specific sampling is needed before a definitive attribution (1).',ao=['B'],sub='LIM',bloom='Evaluate',recall=0)
card('11-028','Discuss a water-supply decision for a settlement needing 100 m³/day: option A delivers 120 m³/day but has failed microbial tests; B delivers 80 m³/day and meets quality tests. Identify a limitation of each and a useful combined response.','A meets volume but needs treatment and safety verification. B is safe in the tests but short by 20 m³/day. Treat and retest A or supplement B with verified safe water while reducing non-essential demand.','A’s volume does not compensate for failed microbial quality (1).|B is short by 20 m³/day; combine with safe treatment/supplementation or demand reduction (1).',ao=['B'],sub='LIM',bloom='Evaluate',recall=0)
card('11-053','Outline the conclusions supported by two acid-rain investigations: lake pH falls from 6.5 to 4.5 while fish survival falls; limestone exposed to pH 4 rain loses 0.8 g while an equal piece in pH 7 rain loses 0.1 g.','The lake record associates acidification with reduced survival but alone does not prove causation. The controlled stone comparison supports greater carbonate damage under acidic conditions.','Lake acidification is associated with lower fish survival, with a causal limitation (1).|Greater limestone loss at pH 4 supports acid damage (1).',ao=['B'],sub='INTERP',bloom='Analyse',recall=0)
# Keep objective command coverage while requiring a derived numerical explanation.
for k in ['2-094','2-096','2-100','2-102','2-106','2-108']:
 CARDS[k]['prompt']=CARDS[k]['prompt'].replace('Determine ','Describe how to determine ',1)
for k in ['10-059','10-078']:CARDS[k]['prompt']=CARDS[k]['prompt'].replace('Suggest ','Outline your choice of ',1)
# One-mark prompts now ask for one credited response rather than two unrelated facts.
card('3-028','State the material heated with limestone to make cement in the syllabus description.','Clay.','Clay (1).',recall=1)
card('3-040','State one carbon-based material associated with nanotechnology.','Graphene or a fullerene.','Graphene or fullerenes; one example required (1).',recall=1)
card('9-013','Name one metal that can occur native.','Gold or platinum.','Gold or platinum; one example required (1).',recall=1)
card('9-041','Name one use of stainless steel.','Cutlery or chemical-processing equipment.','Cutlery or chemical-processing equipment (1).',recall=1)
card('9-042','State one use of zinc other than galvanising iron.','Making brass.','Making brass (1).',recall=1)
card('9-043','State the name of an alloy made using tin.','Bronze.','Bronze (1).',recall=1)
card('9-045','Name one syllabus use of uranium.','Fuel for nuclear power generation.','Nuclear power generation; accept the other syllabus-listed use (1).',recall=1)
card('11-055','State a greenhouse gas other than carbon dioxide whose increase contributes to climate change.','Methane.','Methane (1).',recall=1)
card('7-060','Suggest a soluble reagent to add to barium chloride solution to prepare barium sulfate by precipitation.','Sodium sulfate solution or another suitable soluble sulfate.','A suitable soluble sulfate solution, e.g. sodium sulfate (1).',ao=['A'],sub='FEATURE',bloom='Remember',recall=1)
# Additional practical diagnosis, including explicitly anticipated (not examiner-attributed) misconceptions.
new(1,'1.4-01','Explain the error in reporting the mean of 12.1 g and 12.3 g as 12.200000 g from a balance reading to 0.1 g.','The mean is 12.2 g; the extra zeros claim precision the readings do not support.','Mean 12.2 g (1).|Extra decimal places are not supported by 0.1 g readings (1).',sub='MISCON')
new(12,'12.4-01','Explain the error in calling a mass measurement accurate solely because three repeats all give 6.20 g, when the reference mass is 6.00 g.','The repeats are precise, but are consistently 0.20 g above the reference, so they are not accurate.','Agreement demonstrates precision, not accuracy (1).|Positive 0.20 g deviation demonstrates the accuracy error (1).',sub='MISCON',ao='C',bloom='Analyse')
# Convert a memorised isotope comparison to a real calculation and judgement.
card('2-034','Discuss whether samples X and Y contain the same element and whether mass numbers alone prove radioactivity. X nuclei contain 6 protons and 7 neutrons; Y contain 6 protons and 8 neutrons.','Both are carbon, with nucleon numbers 13 and 14. The neutron difference establishes isotopes; a mass number alone does not establish whether a nucleus is radioactive without stability evidence.','Same six protons, with mass numbers 13 and 14 (1).|Isotopes; mass numbers alone do not prove radioactivity without further evidence (1).',ao=['B'],sub='INTERP',bloom='Analyse',recall=0)
card('2-058','Describe the conclusions from a catalyst trial: without solid X a reaction takes 80 s; with X it takes 30 s at the same temperature. X is recovered unchanged in chemical composition. Does the shorter time prove that more product forms?','X accelerates the reaction and is chemically unchanged at the end, consistent with a catalyst. Time alone does not establish final product amount.','Faster reaction with X chemically unchanged supports catalysis (1).|Shorter time does not establish greater final yield (1).',ao=['B'],sub='INTERP',bloom='Analyse',recall=0)
